<p>Here are my comments as part of the <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/young-and-under-30">Young and Under 30 Project</a>.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>What works well:</strong> The basic idea of &quot;spooky thing that appears behind you&quot; is fertile ground for skips, and the fact that the particular thing that each person sees reflects their mental state provides a narrative structure. The writing itself is technically fine for the most part.</p>
<p><strong>What could be improved:</strong> This skip gets far too bogged down in the mechanics of the anomaly (how long it lasts, its link to the centre of mass), spending a lot of words on those things when they aren't critical to the image or the story of the skip. This is such a staple horror image that bringing an engaging approach to it is important, and this article does nothing much with the basic trope - you don't even give a vivid description of the apparitions.</p>
<p>Several things appear tacked on. I'm broadly okay with including an otherwise-irrelevant recovery log for the purposes of crosslinking, but the self-harm and suicide angle appears to be there just to add danger. The lucid dream angle is never followed up to any effect (you even mention a dream but don't go on to describe it). And the Foundation's torture of a political prisoner comes across as gratuitous.</p>
<p>On that note, the dialogue in the testing logs doesn't feel immersive - while interruptions and repetition is probably more realistic, it creates roadbumps for the reader which can jar them out of the story, and I think that's what happens here.</p>
<p>Finally, I am quite uncomfortable with your depiction of the psyche of a person with schizophrenia as a constantly changing shape with spooky red eyes - that comes across as a very cliched view of schizophrenia, and it's made worse by the self-harm at the end of the test.</p>
<p><strong>How to improve it:</strong> If the narrative is intended to be a visual representation of the mind, streamline the skip to focus on that. Get rid of the description that is too focused on mechanics, and slim down the test logs to that effect as well as cutting unnecessary dialogue. Then work out what set of psyches you'd like to exhibit, and how to arrange them to give escalation across the tests.</p>
<p><strong>Verdict:</strong> Downvoted. This is a solid basic concept that is tripping up over its execution.</p>
<hr />
